## Reading the scan

`crimes scan src/billing.ts` returns repo-wide findings (several detectors are repo-scoped), so the loudest date findings in the output — `Half-UTC, Half-Local`, `Timezone Roulette`, `DST-Naive Day Math`, all **high/medium in `src/date.ts`** — are the obvious suspects. I checked before believing them: **`src/billing.ts` has no imports at all**, so it never calls `date.ts`. Those findings are real, but they can't explain a failure in the billing tests unless the failing test itself exercises `date.ts` helpers. Worth confirming from the failing test name before spending time there.

Filtering to findings that actually anchor to `src/billing.ts`:

| id | charge | why it matters here |
|---|---|---|
| `crime_00013` | Temporal Recklessness (medium) | 7 wall-clock reads: L57, 63, **92**, 198, 238, 250, **273** |
| `crime_00005` | God Function (high) | `generateInvoice` L50–253, 204 lines — the date math is buried at L92 |
| `crime_00025` / `crime_00034` | Test That Proves Nothing | `billing.test.ts` has one test with zero assertions and one `toBeTruthy()` |

## Most likely cause

**L92 in `generateInvoice`, plus its copy at L273 in `recommendUpgrade`:**

```ts
const daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));
if (user.plan !== "free") {
  if (daysSince > 365)      discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.1);
  else if (daysSince > 180) discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.05);
  else if (daysSince > 90)  discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.02);
}
```

This is an **elapsed-ms bucket compared against calendar-day thresholds**, evaluated against an unpinnable `Date.now()`. It produces exactly the failure shape described:

- If the fixture's `user.createdAt` is a fixed timestamp, `daysSince` grows by one every 24h of real time. A fixture seeded to sit near 90/180/365 days passes for a while, then silently crosses the boundary and the asserted total changes — "flaky," but really time-dependent.
- If the fixture computes `createdAt` as "180 days ago" via its own ms arithmetic, the two `Math.floor` truncations land on either side of the `> 180` boundary depending on what fraction of a day has elapsed at the moment the test runs — a genuine intermittent, TZ-independent, worst near the rounding edge.
- `Math.floor(ms / 86_400_000)` is elapsed 24h periods, not calendar days. Any input derived from local wall-clock dates shifts by an hour across a DST transition and flips the bucket for a day or two each spring/autumn.

The discount feeds `subtotal → total`, so a one-day bucket flip changes the invoice total — the classic "same test, different number" boundary failure.

Two amplifiers the scan flagged, both real contributors:

- **`crime_00005`** — the math is line 92 of a 204-line function that also does pricing, tax, logging, metrics, email composition and a retry loop. Nothing about the function signature says its output depends on the current time, so nobody thinks to pin the clock.
- **`crime_00013`'s duplication** — L92 and L273 are the same computation written twice. Fixing one leaves the other drifting, and this is precisely the case `crimes` calls out: an agent editing `generateInvoice` won't load `recommendUpgrade` into context.
- **`crime_00025` / `crime_00034`** — `billing.test.ts` currently asserts nothing meaningful (`renderEnterprisePlan()` with no `expect`, and `toBeTruthy()` on a hardcoded stub). Whatever suite is failing, this file provides no regression net for the fix.

Also non-deterministic in the same function, though not a date issue: `const id = "inv_" + Math.random()...` at L57 — that will break any snapshot assertion on the invoice object.

## Proposed fix

**1. Inject the clock** (`crimes` suggested action `inject_clock`, risk: low) — make the time dependency visible in the signature:

```ts
export interface Clock { now(): number }
export const systemClock: Clock = { now: () => Date.now() };

export function generateInvoice(
  user: User,
  items: InvoiceLine[],
  paymentMethod?: string,
  clock: Clock = systemClock,
): Invoice {
  const createdAt = clock.now();
  // …every Date.now() / new Date() in the body reads from `clock`
```

**2. Extract the boundary math once, with explicit calendar semantics** — this is the actual bug fix, not just testability:

```ts
const MS_PER_DAY = 86_400_000;

/** Whole calendar days elapsed, computed in UTC so DST can't move the boundary. */
export function daysSinceSignup(createdAtMs: number, nowMs: number): number {
  const startOfDay = (ms: number) => Math.floor(ms / MS_PER_DAY);
  return startOfDay(nowMs) - startOfDay(createdAtMs);
}
```

Call it from both L92 and L273 so `recommendUpgrade` and `generateInvoice` can't disagree. Anchoring both endpoints to UTC day starts makes the result depend only on the calendar date, not on the time of day the test happens to run — which removes the intermittency at its source.

**3. Pin the clock in the test and assert the boundary on both sides** — replace the assertion-free tests, and test the edges deliberately rather than hoping the wall clock stays away from them:

```ts
const fixedNow = Date.UTC(2026, 5, 1);
const clock: Clock = { now: () => fixedNow };

it.each([
  [90,  0],      // at 90 days: no discount (threshold is > 90)
  [91,  0.02],
  [180, 0.02],
  [181, 0.05],
  [365, 0.05],
  [366, 0.10],
])("applies %i-day loyalty tier", (days, rate) => {
  const user = { ...proUser, createdAt: fixedNow - days * MS_PER_DAY };
  expect(generateInvoice(user, items, "card", clock).discountCents)
    .toBe(Math.round(expectedSubtotal * rate));
});
```

**Ordering:** do (2) first — it's the defect. (1) is what lets you prove it. (3) is what stops it regressing. All three are `effort: small` per the scan.

If the failing test turns out to name one of the `date.ts` helpers (`todayKey`, `dateLabel`, `christmasMorning`), the diagnosis changes to `crime_00010` — `dateLabel()` mixes `getUTCFullYear()` with `getMonth()`/`getDate()` on the same `Date`, which is a genuine host-offset boundary bug that fails only for hosts west of UTC in the last hours of a day. Check the test name first; the two causes need different fixes.
